The base case of the recursion is when the `permutation` list has reached the length of the `nums` array, in which case we add the permutation to the `result` list and return.

In the recursive case, we iterate over the elements in `nums` that haven't been used yet and add them to the `permutation` list. We then recurse on `permuteHelper` to generate the next permutation. After the recursive call returns, we remove the last element from the `permutation` list and set the `used` position back to `false`.

Finally, when all permutations have been generated, we return the `result` list.

