It seems that the question is asking for help with implementing a solution to <https://leetcode.com/problems/single-number-ii/> However, LeetCode requires a premium subscription to access the editorials, but I can still explain the idea behind the question.
The problem is asking to find the unique element in an array of distinct elements, where every element appears three times except for one element
The brute force solution would be to loop through the entire array and find the element that does not satisfy the condition of either appearing three times or appearing only one time. But this would take a long time complexity of O(n^2), which is too high.
The optimal solution is to use the fact that every element appears three times except for one element to find the unique element. Essentially, if every element appears three times except for one element, then the sum of all the elements in the array (or xor of all the elements in the array) with a bitwise and of 1 will result in the unique element (the one that does not appear three times). Essentially, if you do a bitwise and of 1 with a number that appears three times, the result is 0 (the bitwise and of 1 with 3 is 0, for example). However, if you do a bitwise and of 1 with a number that appears only once, the result is 1 (the bitwise and of 1 with 5 is 1, for example). So, we can apply the above knowledge to find the unique element in the array.
We create an integer array count of size 32, and iterate over the input array nums. For every element, we bitshift it 32 times, and update the count array with the bitwise and of 1 with the nums[i] and i. The count array essentially counts how many times each bit appears in the input array. After this, we iterate over the count array and if a count is not divisible by 3, we bitwise or it with a 1 shifted by the index of the count array, and return the result as the unique element. If there is no remainder, we return 0.

